---
import AnatomyDiagram from '../components/AnatomyDiagram.astro';
import Glossary from '../components/Glossary.astro';
import SourcesAndTips from '../components/SourcesAndTips.astro';
import TaxonomyLadder from '../components/TaxonomyLadder.astro';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const sources = [
  {
    label: 'World Spider Catalog, Natural History Museum Bern — current spider taxonomy and totals',
    url: 'https://wsc.nmbe.ch/',
    kind: 'database' as const,
  },
  {
    label: 'Burke Museum — Spider Myths: "Spiders are insects"',
    url: 'https://www.burkemuseum.org/collections-and-research/biology/arachnology-and-entomology/spider-myths/myth-spiders-are-insects',
    kind: 'institution' as const,
  },
  {
    label: 'Museum of New Zealand Te Papa Tongarewa — What is a bug? Insects, arachnids and myriapods',
    url: 'https://tepapa.govt.nz/discover-collections/read-watch-play/science-and-nature/spiders-and-insects/your-bug-questions/what',
    kind: 'institution' as const,
  },
  {
    label: 'araneae — Spiders of Europe, identification and taxonomy',
    url: 'https://araneae.nmbe.ch/',
    kind: 'database' as const,
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Where jumping spiders fit — spoodHub"
  description="A plain-language guide to how spiders are classified, why they are not insects or bugs, and where jumping spiders sit among them."
>
  <a href="/species" class="text-sm text-accent hover:underline">← All species</a>

  <header class="mt-4">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold">Where jumping spiders fit</h1>
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-lg text-muted">
      What a jumping spider actually is, why it is not a bug or an insect, and how the names on
      the species pages here relate to each other.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="mt-12">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Is a spider a bug?</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-muted">
      No — twice over, depending on how strict you want to be.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-muted">
      <strong class="text-fg">A "bug", strictly speaking, is a very specific thing:</strong> an
      insect in the order Hemiptera, with piercing-sucking mouthparts shaped into a beak. Aphids,
      cicadas, shield bugs and bed bugs are true bugs. By that definition a beetle is not a bug,
      a butterfly is not a bug, and a spider is nowhere close.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-muted">
      <strong class="text-fg">A spider is not an insect either.</strong> Both are arthropods, but
      they part company above that. The Burke Museum puts the distance well: spiders are
      <em>"as distant from insects as birds are from fish."</em> The two lineages separated more
      than 500 million years ago.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-muted">
      In everyday speech "bug" just means "small thing with legs", and nobody needs correcting at
      a dinner party. But on a site about spider care the distinction earns its keep, because it
      is why insect advice — insecticides especially — does not transfer.
    </p>

    <AnatomyDiagram />

    {/* No min-width and no overflow wrapper: cells wrap instead of the table
        scrolling sideways. Text is kept short enough to survive a narrow phone. */}
    <table class="mt-4 w-full table-fixed border-collapse text-xs sm:text-sm">
      <thead>
        <tr class="border-b border-line text-left">
          <th class="w-[30%] py-2 pr-2 font-medium sm:pr-4"></th>
          <th class="w-[35%] py-2 pr-2 font-medium sm:pr-4">Insect</th>
          <th class="w-[35%] py-2 font-medium">Spider</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="text-muted [word-break:break-word]">
        {
          [
            ['Body parts', '3 — head, thorax, abdomen', '2 — cephalothorax, abdomen'],
            ['Legs', '6', '8'],
            ['Antennae', '2', 'None'],
            ['Eyes', '2 compound', 'Usually 8 simple'],
            ['Wings', '4, 2, or none', 'Never'],
            ['Abdomen', 'Segmented', 'Unsegmented'],
            ['Silk', 'Some, from the mouth', 'From rear spinnerets'],
          ].map(([label, insect, spider]) => (
            <tr class="border-b border-line/60 align-top">
              <td class="py-2 pr-2 font-medium text-fg sm:pr-4">{label}</td>
              <td class="py-2 pr-2 sm:pr-4">{insect}</td>
              <td class="py-2">{spider}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-14">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">From "animal" down to your spider</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-muted">
      Classification is a set of nested boxes, each one narrower than the last. Here is the whole
      path for a regal jumping spider — the species most people in this hobby keep.
    </p>

    <TaxonomyLadder />

    <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-muted">
      Totals from the World Spider Catalog, which is revised constantly — roughly three new
      species are described every day, so treat these as approximate. Figures here are as of
      early 2026.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-14">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">The two kinds of spider</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-muted">
      The split that matters most in the pet hobby happens at the infraorder level, and it comes
      down to which way the fangs move.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-5 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-accent/40 bg-accent/5 p-5">
        <h3 class="font-semibold">Araneomorphae</h3>
        <p class="text-xs text-muted">"True spiders" — the vast majority</p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm">
          Fangs point toward each other and <strong>pinch sideways</strong>, like a pair of
          tongs. Generally smaller, shorter-lived, and faster to mature.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-muted">
          Jumping spiders, orb weavers, wolf spiders, house spiders — and everything on this
          site.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-line bg-surface p-5">
        <h3 class="font-semibold">Mygalomorphae</h3>
        <p class="text-xs text-muted">Tarantulas and their relatives</p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm">
          Fangs point straight down and <strong>strike in parallel</strong>, like a pickaxe.
          Often much larger and dramatically longer-lived — some females reach 20–30 years.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-muted">
          Tarantulas (family Theraphosidae), trapdoor spiders, funnel-webs.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-5 text-muted">
      So a tarantula is a spider, but it is not a close relative of a jumping spider — the two
      sit on different branches, and almost no husbandry advice transfers between them. A care
      sheet written for a tarantula is not a starting point for a jumping spider.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-14">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Genus, species, and why it matters here</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-muted">
      Every species has a two-part name: the <strong class="text-fg">genus</strong> first,
      capitalised, then the <strong class="text-fg">species</strong>, lowercase. Both go in
      italics. So <em>Phidippus regius</em> is the species <em>regius</em> within the genus
      <em>Phidippus</em>.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-5 rounded-xl border border-line bg-surface p-5">
      <h3 class="font-semibold">A worked example from this site</h3>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-muted">
        The sleep guide cites a study done on <em>Evarcha arcuata</em>, while you probably keep
        <em>Phidippus regius</em>. Both are jumping spiders — same family, Salticidae. But they
        are different <em>genera</em>, roughly the distance between a lynx and a housecat: close
        enough that shared biology is a fair expectation, far enough that it is an expectation
        rather than a finding.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-muted">
        That is why guides here name the species a claim came from. "Jumping spiders do X" hides
        a lot when the family has nearly 7,000 species in it.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h3 class="mt-8 text-lg font-semibold">What about subspecies?</h3>
    <p class="mt-2 text-muted">
      Rarely used in spiders. Modern arachnology has mostly either promoted old subspecies to
      full species or folded them into one — so for almost everything you will meet in the hobby,
      the two-part name is the whole story.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-muted">
      What you <em>will</em> meet is <strong class="text-fg">trade names and locality names</strong>,
      which are not taxonomy at all. Sellers use labels like "Bahia" or "Florida orange" for
      color forms of the same species. They can be genuinely useful for knowing what a spider
      will look like, but they carry no scientific status and are not consistent between sellers.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-muted">
      Names also change. Species get moved between genera as relationships are worked out, which
      is why the World Spider Catalog is the reference to check rather than an older care sheet.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-14">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">What makes a jumping spider a jumping spider</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-muted">
      Salticidae is the largest spider family — around 6,950 species in roughly 695 genera,
      nearly one in eight of all spiders. What sets them apart:
    </p>
    <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-muted">
      <li class="flex gap-3">
        <span class="text-accent">▪</span>
        <span>
          <strong class="text-fg">Exceptional eyesight.</strong> The two huge forward-facing eyes
          give acute, image-forming vision unmatched among spiders of their size. They see you
          looking at them.
        </span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-3">
        <span class="text-accent">▪</span>
        <span>
          <strong class="text-fg">They stalk instead of trapping.</strong> No prey-catching web.
          They hunt by sight, stalk, and pounce — which is why enclosure layout and light matter
          so much more than they would for a web-builder.
        </span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-3">
        <span class="text-accent">▪</span>
        <span>
          <strong class="text-fg">Silk is safety equipment, not a trap.</strong> A dragline is
          anchored before jumping, and silk is used for resting retreats and egg sacs.
        </span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-3">
        <span class="text-accent">▪</span>
        <span>
          <strong class="text-fg">Hydraulic legs.</strong> Jumps are powered by body fluid
          pressure rather than muscle alone.
        </span>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <div class="mt-8 rounded-xl border border-line bg-raised p-5">
      {/* The {' '} are load-bearing: Astro collapses the newline between inline
          text and an element, so without them this renders as
          "specifics?Browse the species profilesor start with the…" */}
      <p class="text-sm text-muted">
        Ready for the specifics?{' '}
        <a href="/species" class="text-accent hover:underline">Browse the species profiles</a>{' '}
        or start with the{' '}
        <a href="/care" class="text-accent hover:underline">care guides</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Glossary />

  <SourcesAndTips sources={sources} tips={[]} />
</Base>
